WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.840
A seabird takes in seawater, or a heavy load of salt.

2
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:14.260
Seawater is near 1,000 milliosmoles per kilogram, an approximate figure.

3
00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:18.540
That counts dissolved particles in each kilogram of water.

4
00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.920
Bird kidneys cannot make urine as salty as the sea.

5
00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.780
So where does the extra salt go?

6
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:32.810
Marine birds such as cormorants and gulls often feed at sea, where fresh water is

7
00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:36.030
scarce. Think about the kidney route alone.

8
00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:41.630
A bird that cleared the salt of seawater through its kidneys alone would need more

9
00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:42.730
water than it drank.

10
00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.770
The kidney route alone leaves the bird short of water.

11
00:00:47.583 --> 00:00:55.423
In 1958, Knut Schmidt-Nielsen, C. B. Jørgensen and H. Osaki tested whether another

12
00:00:55.423 --> 00:01:01.703
route existed. They gave double-crested cormorants a load of salt, by mouth or by

13
00:01:01.703 --> 00:01:04.883
infusion. Then they collected two fluids.

14
00:01:05.623 --> 00:01:08.583
One was the cloacal fluid, urine mixed with feces.

15
00:01:09.603 --> 00:01:12.343
The other was a clear fluid that dripped from the beak.

16
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:16.183
Paired nasal salt glands above the eyes made it.

17
00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:22.837
If these birds handled salt the way mammals do, the cloacal fluid would carry the

18
00:01:22.837 --> 00:01:30.537
load. It did not. After the load, the nasal fluid held about 500 to 600 millimoles

19
00:01:30.537 --> 00:01:32.337
per liter of sodium chloride.

20
00:01:32.957 --> 00:01:36.057
That is similar to seawater, or somewhat above it.

21
00:01:36.737 --> 00:01:40.497
The glands put out about 10 times as much salt as the cloaca.

22
00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:47.707
A gland that excretes salt more concentrated than seawater changes the water budget.

23
00:01:48.527 --> 00:01:53.767
A bird can get rid of the salt of a gulp of seawater in less water than the gulp

24
00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:55.807
held. It keeps the difference.

25
00:01:56.927 --> 00:01:59.927
Gulls reach even higher values than these cormorants did.

26
00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:02.510
What did the experiment show?

27
00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:08.310
It ruled in a route outside the kidneys, through which these birds can excrete a

28
00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:12.430
salt load. It did not show how the gland concentrates salt.

29
00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.090
It did not show that every bird has active salt glands.

30
00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:20.510
Sea turtles and the marine iguana have salt glands too.

31
00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:24.950
In cormorants, salt can leave by a route besides the kidneys.
